<h2>Algebraic K-theory — K₀, K₁, the +-Construction & the Bass-Quillen Conjecture</h2>
<p>Algebraic K-theory is a far-reaching branch of mathematics that assigns a sequence of groups K_n(R) to a ring R, capturing deep invariants of modules over R. It began with the <strong>Grothendieck group</strong> K₀ (projective modules) and <strong>Bass's K₁</strong> (the general linear group modulo elementary matrices). <strong>Quillen's +-construction</strong> gave higher K-groups K_n(R) = π_n(BGL(R)⁺) for n ≥ 1, and the <strong>Q-construction</strong> extended K-theory to exact categories. The <strong>Brown-Gersten spectral sequence</strong> links K-theory to Chow groups via the coniveau filtration. The <strong>Gersten conjecture</strong> predicts that the coniveau spectral sequence degenerates at E₂ for regular local rings. The <strong>Bass-Quillen conjecture</strong> concerns the K-theory of polynomial rings. The <strong>Merkurjev-Suslin theorem</strong> identifies K₂ with the Brauer group. The <strong>Lichtenbaum-Quillen conjectures</strong> relate algebraic K-theory with finite coefficients to étale cohomology, culminating in <strong>Voevodsky's proof</strong> of the Bloch-Kato conjecture using motivic homotopy theory.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"K-theory is the algebraic topology of regular rings."</div><div class="attribution">— Daniel Quillen</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>📏 K₀ and K₁</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Classical</div><div class="story-title">The Grothendieck Group, K₁, and the Whitehead Lemma</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Grothendieck group</strong> K₀(R) is the abelian group generated by isomorphism classes [P] of finitely generated projective R-modules modulo the relation [P⊕Q] = [P] + [Q]. For a principal ideal domain (PID), K₀(R) = ℤ via the rank map. The <strong>reduced K₀</strong> is ker(K₀(R) → ℤ). For a Dedekind domain, K₀(R) = ℤ ⊕ Cl(R) where Cl(R) is the ideal class group. <strong>K₁(R)</strong> = GL(R)/E(R) where GL(R) = lim→ GL_n(R) is the infinite general linear group and E(R) = lim→ E_n(R) is the subgroup of elementary matrices (those differing from the identity by a single off-diagonal entry). The <strong>Whitehead lemma</strong>: E(R) is the commutator subgroup of GL(R), hence K₁(R) = GL(R)^{ab}, the abelianization. For a field F, K₁(F) = F^×. The K-theory of integers: K₀(ℤ) = ℤ, K₁(ℤ) = ℤ/2. The <strong>Bass-Schanuel formula</strong> computes K₀ of group rings ℤ[G] in terms of the finiteness obstruction for groups.</div><div class="story-moral">K₀ measures projective modules via the Grothendieck construction; K₁ measures units and determinant-like invariants via the general linear group.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🌍 Quillen's +-Construction and Higher K-theory</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">+-Construction</div><div class="story-title">The Plus Construction, Higher K-Groups, and the Q-Construction</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>+-construction</strong>: given a perfect normal subgroup P ⊂ π₁(X) (the maximal perfect subgroup), there exists a space X⁺ and a map X → X⁺ such that H_*(X⁺) = H_*(X) and π₁(X⁺) = π₁(X)/P. Apply to X = BGL(R) and P = E(R) (the elementary subgroup, which is perfect for n ≥ 3). Define <strong>K_n(R) = π_n(BGL(R)⁺)</strong> for n ≥ 1. This yields K₁(R) = GL(R)/E(R) (matching Bass) and K₂(R) = H₂(E(R), ℤ) (the <strong>Steinberg group</strong>). The <strong>Q-construction</strong> (Quillen's alternative): for an exact category C, the category QC has objects the same as C and morphisms defined via admissible exact sequences; the classifying space B(QC) has homotopy groups K_n(C) = π_{n+1}(BQC). The <strong>fundamental theorem of K-theory</strong>: K_n(R[t, t⁻¹]) ≅ K_n(R) ⊕ K_{n−1}(R). The <strong>Dévissage theorem</strong>: for an abelian category A where every object has finite length, K(A) ≅ K(A_red) where A_red is the full subcategory of semisimple objects. The <strong>Nesterenko-Suslin theorem</strong>: K_n(R) ≅ π_n(BGL(R)⁺). The <strong>Milnor K-theory</strong> K^M_*(F) for a field F is defined by generators {a₁, …, a_n} with a_i ∈ F^× modulo the Steinberg relation {a, 1−a} = 0, and maps to Quillen K-theory.</div><div class="story-moral">The +-construction kills the elementary subgroup to produce a simply connected space whose homotopy groups define higher K-theory; the Q-construction extends this to exact categories.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🧮 The Coniveau Spectral Sequence and the Gersten Conjecture</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Spectral</div><div class="story-title">The Brown-Gersten Spectral Sequence, Gersten's Conjecture, and the Merkurjev-Suslin Theorem</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>coniveau filtration</strong> on K-theory: F^p K_n(X) = im(K_n(X_p) → K_n(X)) where X_p ranges over subschemes of codimension ≥ p. The <strong>Brown-Gersten spectral sequence</strong>: E₁^{p,q} = ⊕_{x∈X^{(p)}} K_{−p−q}(k(x)) ⇒ K_{−p−q}(X), where X^{(p)} are points of codimension p and k(x) is the residue field. For a <strong>regular local ring</strong>, the <strong>Gersten conjecture</strong> states that the coniveau spectral sequence degenerates at E₂. Equivalently, the <strong>Gersten complex</strong> (or Cousin complex): 0 → K_n(R) → K_n(k(R)) → ⊕_{codim 1} K_{n−1}(k(x)) → ⊕_{codim 2} K_{n−2}(k(x)) → … is exact for regular local rings. This is known for fields (trivially) and for discrete valuation rings (by Quillen). The <strong>Merkurjev-Suslin theorem</strong> (1982): for a field F and integer n invertible in F, K₂(F)/n ≅ Br_n(F), the n-torsion of the Brauer group. The proof uses the <strong>norm residue homomorphism</strong> K^M_2(F)/n → H²_{ét}(F, μ_n^{⊗2}) and shows it is an isomorphism.</div><div class="story-moral">The coniveau spectral sequence organizes K-theory by codimension; the Gersten conjecture predicts it degenerates for regular local rings, giving a resolution by simpler K-groups of fields.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🔥 The Lichtenbaum-Quillen Conjectures and Voevodsky's Proof</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Motivic</div><div class="story-title">The Lichtenbaum-Quillen Conjectures, the Bloch-Kato Conjecture, and Motivic Homotopy Theory</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>Lichtenbaum-Quillen conjecture</strong>: for a scheme X of finite type over ℤ and ℓ invertible in X, the étale K-theory map K(X) → K_ét(X) induces an isomorphism on homotopy groups with finite ℓ-adic coefficients after completion. Equivalently: K_n(X; ℤ/ℓ) ≅ H^{n−i}_{ét}(X, ℤ_ℓ(i)) for appropriate i. The <strong>motivic spectral sequence</strong> (Atiyah-Hirzebruch for K-theory): E₂^{p,q} = H^{p−q}(X, ℤ(−q)) ⇒ K_{−p−q}(X), where H^{*,*}(X, ℤ) is motivic cohomology. The <strong>Beilinson-Lichtenbaum conjecture</strong>: H^{p,q}_M(X, ℤ/ℓ) ≅ H^{p}_{ét}(X, μ_ℓ^{⊗q}) for p ≤ q (now a theorem of Voevodsky). The <strong>Bloch-Kato conjecture</strong> (milestone): the norm residue homomorphism K^M_n(F)/ℓ → H^n_{ét}(F, μ_ℓ^{⊗n}) is an isomorphism for all n and ℓ invertible in F. This subsumes the <strong>Milnor conjecture</strong> (n = 2, ℓ = 2: K^M_2(F)/2 ≅ H²_{ét}(F, μ₂^{⊗2}) ≅ Br₂(F)) and the <strong>Merkurjev-Suslin theorem</strong> (n = 2, general ℓ). <strong>Voevodsky's proof</strong> (2003, Fields Medal) uses <strong>motivic homotopy theory</strong>: the Bloch-Kato conjecture follows from the existence of the <strong>motivic Steenrod algebra</strong>, the <strong>Norm Residue theorem</strong>, and the construction of <strong>splitting varieties</strong> (generalized Rost motives). The <strong>Bloch's conjecture</strong> on existence of the Gersten resolution for K₃ of fields was also resolved via these methods.</div><div class="story-moral">The Lichtenbaum-Quillen conjectures identify algebraic K-theory with étale cohomology after finite coefficients; Voevodsky's proof of Bloch-Kato unified Milnor K-theory with Galois cohomology.</div></div>
